 { Here is a recent example from one of my own games. Jacob Aagaard – Kim Pilgaard, Helsingor 2012 } { _ } 29. Qxc1 $2 { I have to admit that I played this rather quickly and on general grounds. I just wanted to keep an eye on the g5- and a6-pawns. I could not really work out what I should be looking at. This is where the method of elimination would have come in handy. If I had been actively looking for the faults with this capture, my intuitively and logically most obvious move, I would have found them rather easily, I hope, and played the alternative. But let’s return to the initial decision, now with the knowledge that the correct move was } ( 29. Rxc1 $1 { which would have allowed White to retain his great positional advantage. After } g6 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rc6 Nb8 32. Rc2 $16 { Black will suffer for a long time and not necessarily live to tell the tale. } ) 
bxa4 30. Rxa4 Nb8 $2 { Kim was a bit low on time and played this with a painful expression on his face. In the commentary room, they spotted a nice equalizing shot, which remained a secret to the players until we went there. } ( 30... a5 $3 { This surprising move exploits the overloading of the white pieces and eliminates Black’s chronic weakness. If White takes with the rook the b4-pawn is lost, and } 31. b5 $2 { just drops a pawn. So, there is no real alternative to } ( 30... Nb8 30. bxa5 Nc5 32. Ra3 { when the most accurate is } Ra7 $1 { after which Black will regain his pawn and achieve a draw. } ) 
 ) 
 { So why did Kim, a strong IM, not find this nice move? The real explanation is that he was not looking for it. Mentally he had already given up. } 31. Qa3 $16 Qb6 32. Bxa6 Nxa6 33. Rxa6 Qd4 $2 { Despite the mistake a few moves back, only this appears to be the decisive error. Black is immediately mated. I was expecting the game to end in a draw with simple play: } ( 33... Qxb4 { Instead, Kim rejected this option because of: } 34. Qxb4 { Now the attack with } ( 33... Qd4 33. Ra8 Kh7 35. Qf3 { does not work because of } Rb8 $1 { and the tactics work in favour of a draw. } ) 
Rxb4 35. f3 $1 { This was indeed my intention; White wins the d6-pawn. But I did not think it would win the game and it appears this is correct. My thinking during the game was not crystal clear, but the main idea was correct. } Rb1 36. Kh2 Kf8 37. Rxd6 Ke7 38. Ra6 Rb2 { I suspect that Black will hold with best play. It is very difficult for White to get the king into the game. If White puts the king on g3, Black will play ...g6 in order to meet Rf1 with ...f7-f5!, preventing Rf2 and preparing to weaken the e4-pawn. Am I sure? No, but does it matter? } ) 
34. Ra8+ Kh7 35. Qf3 $1 { If we forget about Kim’s disillusionment with his position and previous play, we can see that an emotionally neutral player would not have lost in 35 moves. He would have reached the diagram above and then taken it one move at a time. Now, I think that a draw is the correct outcome in the rook ending, but really it is not something that Kim needed to decide during the game. What he did was to eliminate the better of the options on an assumption that it was hopeless and thus blundered straight into an easy mate. Had he used the method of elimination correctly and looked at both his choices, his outcome would have been far better and he would most likely have held the game, despite his assumptions. Let go of assumptions and replace them with calculation techniques. If used with skill, this will give you a better result nine times out of ten. } 
 1-0

