 { The method of elimination is a sort of prophylaxis in that you offer your own move up to your opponent in your mind, and for a moment you look at the position from his side, trying to knock down what you are coming up with. This can be a time-consuming process, but it can also be rather rewarding. The final example is virtually impossible to solve, should you try to do so in any other way than elimination. So, let’s shoot down Black’s moves one by one till we run out of bullets! Lisa Schut – Matthew Sadler, Wijk aan Zee 2012 Let’s start by eliminating what Sadler played in the game: } 45... Kh4 $2 { Other moves are also easy to get rid of: A. } ( 45... Qa1 $2 46. Kg2 Qf6 47. Kxg3 { wins for White. B. } ) 
 ( 45... Bxf3 $2 { is refuted by a nice combination: } 46. Ng7 $3 Kh4 ( 46... Qxg7 47. Qxf3 Kh4 48. Rh6 { with mate to follow. } ) 
47. Qxe7 $1 { and White wins. C. } ) 
 ( 45... h6 $2 46. Nf4 Kg5 47. h4 Kxh4 48. Qxe7 $1 { does not require any further calculation. D. } ) 
 ( 45... Bc8 $2 { This can be refuted by the very human } 46. Nf4 { Or instead by a mating attack: } ( 45... Kh4 45. Rd5 Kg6 47. Qe4 Kf7 48. Qxh7 Kxe6 49. Qg8 Qf7 ( 49... Rf7 50. Qxc8 Ke7 51. Qd8 Ke6 52. Qd6 ) 
50. Qg4 Kf6 51. Qg5 Ke6 52. Qe5 ) 
Kh4 47. Ng2 { protecting the queen before taking the black queen. E. For this reason, the only move is } ) 
 ( 45... Rf7 $3 { preventing the knight check. And after } 46. Rd4 Bxf3 47. Nf4 Kh6 { Black’s position might look exceptionally dodgy, but it is all an illusion. } ) 
 { White has nothing better than a perpetual check. } 46. Rd4+ Kh5 47. Nf4+ Kh6 48. Ne6+ $6 { Trying to repeat the position. It would have been better to first bring the queen to safety with } ( 48. Qd2 $1 { when Black would struggle to come up with a response to Nd5+. There are a few lines, but we can quickly determine that Black is lost. } Re8 ( 48. Ne6+ Qg5 49. Rd6 { wins the queen immediately. The computer defence is } ) 
 ( 48. Ne6+ Re1 $5 49. Kg2 Bxf3 50. Kxf3 Rf1 { But White wins with either } 51. Ke3 { Or } ( 51. Ke4 Qe7 52. Ne6 Kg6 53. Rd6 { and the extra piece is permanent. } ) 
 ) 
49. Nd5 Qg5 50. Rh4 { and White wins. } ) 
Kg6 $5 { Sadler had probably spotted White’s win on the previous move and maybe feared that White just wanted to repeat before executing it. } 49. Rg4+ $2 { This wins the queen, but not the game. Both } ( 49. Rd6 { And } ) 
 ( 49. Qd3 Kh6 50. Qd2 Kg6 51. Rg4 Kf7 52. Nd8 { were reasonably simple wins, but I assume that White was running out of time and nervous energy. } ) 
Kf7 50. Rf4 Rxe6 51. Rxf6+ Rxf6 52. Qd3 Bxf3 53. Qxh7+ Kf8 54. Qxa7 g2 55. Qb8+ Kf7 56. Qc7+ Kf8 57. Qd8+ Kf7 58. Qd7+ Kf8 59. Qd4 Kf7 60. Kh2 Bc6 61. Qxb6 Rf1 62. Qc7+ Kf8 63. Qd8+ Kf7 64. Qc7+ Kf8 65. Qc8+ { Elimination is a great tool when you have many options and it is likely several of them do not work. Used incorrectly it can lead to poor decisions, but used properly it can solve problems that might otherwise seem entirely impossible. } 
 1/2-1/2

